Verify that comdb2_locks.client_id joins a lock back to the client that took it.

The role says which table to join: 'R' locks join comdb2_connections, 'W' locks
join comdb2_active_osqls.  client_id is derived locally on each side -- from the
connection id for a read, from the session uuid for a write -- so the join works
whether or not anything was sent over the wire.

osql_send_clientinfo carries the client taskname and pid to the master, which is
the only way comdb2_active_osqls can report argv0 and pid for a BPLOG row; the
master already knows the origin host from the session.  With the tunable off
those two columns are NULL and the join still works.

'A' locks report no client_id: a replicant takes a transaction's locks up front,
off the commit record, before any record naming a client is applied.

comdb2_replication covers the replicant instead -- a row per thread applying,
naming the client from the txn's log record (log_clientinfo) and the statement
that thread is on.  Clustered runs only; a single node applies nothing.

The last check flushes and restarts a node, so recovery has to read the
clientinfo record back through handle_clientinfo -- a path none of the
live-state checks touch.
